alidation", "stage": "planner/1", "status": "matched", "expected_prompt_tokens": 3660, "observed_prompt_tokens": 3660, "tokenization_request_sha256": "b109d24e0c7a4d4b5a52b60730aa8b7746073c455edc6a4c00d7d565e1102447", "sequence": 7, "elapsed_seconds": 13.881144046783447}, {"event": "reasoning_response", "operation": "planner", "call_index": 1, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Plan an executable dependency DAG of 1 to 6 tasks using only the question.\nReturn steps with question, output_slot, answer_type, inputs, execution, plus final_node_id.\nDo not answer, guess intermediate entities, or use outside knowledge. Preserve all entity,\nyear, relation and scope constraints. Ground later queries with {parent_slot} placeholders.\nexecution=retrieval obtains missing factual evidence; execution=compose combines supported\nparent conclusions and their necessary sources. Compose tasks must declare their necessary\nparents. Include the final comparison or synthesis in this same plan when needed.\nfinal_node_id identifies the existing task that answers the requested question completely.\nfinal_node_id MUST exactly equal that task's output_slot string. Never use a positional\nname such as step2 unless step2 is literally the declared output_slot. For example,\nif the final task has output_slot=\"city\", return final_node_id=\"city\".\nFor a natural chain such as director -> university -> city, city is the final task; do not\nappend a redundant answer task. The final task has no children. Independent tasks are allowed.\nUse unique simple output slots, no cycles, no hidden extra nodes. \nAlso return output_contract and program. Before ANY retrieval, plan every foreseeable\ndownstream task, its input dependencies and the designated terminal. Unknown entities,\nrules and conditions are UNBOUND INPUTS, not grounds to omit downstream tasks.\nF is the typed solving relation of this DAG; a reduced F_t never replaces the DAG.\noutput_contract has mode(value/one_reason/all_failures/semantic), result_type\n(bool/entity/number/record/set/text), entity_scope, time_scope and explanation\n(none/one/all/semantic), optional fields. Do not reduce the user's output requirements.\nprogram has variables (ID -> declaration), expression, optional constraints,\nrules_complete and rule_binding. Declaration has type, demand (a planned task ID),\noptional domain, open, unit, scope. Variables may share a task for bounded multi-field reading.\nExplicitly declare each variable's scope={entity_scope,time_scope} BEFORE retrieval,\nusing the meaning of that variable's own planned task, not the final answer's scope.\nFor example, a historical observation and a future recommendation have different time\nscopes; compared entities may differ from the final comparison's entity scope.\nThe scope of every terminal-task variable MUST equal the immutable output_contract scope.\nSupported parents retain their own scopes when used or audited by another task; never\nrelabel historical evidence as current/future evidence to satisfy the output scope.\nOmitting scope is only compatibility for old plans and inherits output_contract scope.\nOmit optional fields that do not apply; optional does not mean fill with null.\nAn unknown/unrestricted domain is represented by OMITTING domain, not null, a string,\na scalar, or an empty array. For an open entity/text variable use, for example,\n{\"type\":\"entity\",\"demand\":\"place\",\"open\":true} with no domain field.\nA known finite domain is an array of correctly typed possible values; only use\nvalues explicitly constrained by the question, never guessed answers or candidates.\nOnly number variables may use an interval domain: {\"lower\":0,\"upper\":null}.\nInterval endpoints are numbers or null (unbounded); lower_closed/upper_closed are\noptional booleans. Null endpoints are legal; a null domain itself is not.\nA numeric domain states legal possible values, NOT an acceptance threshold. Keep\nthresholds in comparison expressions, never exclude a possible failing observation.\nUnknown numeric bounds and memory-derived rules must remain unknown until sourced.\nExpressions ONLY use {op:const,value:...}, {op:var,id:...}, {op:and/or/not/eq/ne/lt/le/gt/ge/if/tuple,args:[...]},\nor {op:record/failures/one_reason,fields:{name:expression}}. No code.\nUse symbolic value mode for ordinary sourced facts/entity chains and comparisons.\nFor genuinely open advice use semantic mode, with the terminal text variable as expression.\none_reason and all_failures are ONLY Boolean-witness operators over named conditions:\none_reason requires expression={op:one_reason,fields:{name:boolean_expression,...}}\nand result_type=record; its result has exactly decision and reason. all_failures\nrequires expression={op:failures,fields:{name:boolean_expression,...}} and result_type=set;\nits result lists all failed condition names. These modes do NOT mean an ordinary\nnatural-language explanation, a historical reason, or a list of personalized reasons.\nUse value for sourced explanation text/lists, or semantic for an open synthesis.\nThe expression's declared result type must match output_contract.result_type,\nincluding an initial rule-bound placeholder; tuple expressions produce arrays (set).\nFor symbolic modes without a rule_binding completion route, rules_complete=false\nis allowed only for necessary-condition and/one_reason programs that can certify\na negative witness; it can never certify a positive or exhaustive result.\nSemantic mode is assessed from supported evidence and never receives a symbolic\ncompleteness certificate. Do not mark rules complete\nunless their completeness is justified by the task or applicable original evidence.\nrules_complete describes the completeness of the solving relation/decision rules,\nNOT corpus completeness, retrieval coverage, confidence, or whether factual input\nvalues are already known. For a direct sourced fact/entity lookup, or a fixed\ncomparison/composition whose full function is specified by the question, the\nsolving relation is complete even while all its factual inputs are still unbound:\nset rules_complete=true (or omit it). Missing facts remain unknown variables and\nrequire sources. Use false only when applicable decision rules themselves must\nstill be obtained from memory through rule_binding, or for the explicit permitted\nnegative-witness case. Never invent thresholds, source values, or completeness.\nRules from memory: do NOT invent thresholds. Plan a rule task and all foreseeable\ncondition-checking tasks with that rule as an unbound parent. Set rules_complete=false.\nrule_binding={variable:rule_variable,condition_demands:[preplanned task IDs]} permits a\nsourced rule reading to instantiate internal conditions within those tasks. Initially\nuse an unbound result variable in the expression. Keep the output contract fixed.\nEvery variable's demand must exist; parameter dependencies use the DAG inputs.\nThe terminal may be compose even when some parents will later be short-circuited.\nOptional program.task_expressions maps intermediate compose variables to whitelist\nexpressions over declared earlier DAG parents. These are free deterministic bindings;\npartial evaluation runs before demanding every planned parent. Do not use this to\ninvent sourced facts or create paid tasks. Unsupported compose remains semantic reading.\n\n\nFor a personalized query, relevant preferences, past experiences, dislikes,\nreasons, historical stages and constraints are evidence even when they do not repeat the new event.\nA recommendation needs evidence of the user's tastes; the history need not already contain the recommendation.\nA conversational update is not a request to discover unspecified event names, dates, locations or schedules.\nRequire these details only if actually requested. Adapt to the query: do not impose a change/reason template\non every question. Preserve uncertain cross-domain connections as implicit/partial, not explicit facts.\nDo not treat an assistant suggestion as a user preference or observation order as calendar event time.\n\nType consistency is structural: a var expression has its variable declaration's\ntype; record produces record; tuple produces set (a JSON array), never string\nconcatenation; comparisons and and/or/not produce bool. If branches must have\nthe same result type. Unknown facts stay unbound; the partial evaluator handles\nunknowns. Do not test unknownness with eq/ne against const:null, supply an\n\"Unknown\" string fallback, or use an if branch that omits requested fields.\nA string const has type entity, NOT text. Comparing a text variable to a string\nconst such as \"Unknown\" is invalid, as is combining a text branch with that\nentity-valued fallback. Missing factual input is absence of a binding, never\nnull or a sentinel value to test. Do not move illegal guards into constraints or\ntask_expressions: the same typing rules apply there. Direct fact records need\nonly an unconditional record of unbound var references, with no availability\nchecks; the controller waits for supported bindings before publishing.\nThe complete expression must match output_contract.result_type. Preserve every\nrequested output field, dependency and scope when correcting a plan; do not\ncoerce values or narrow the user's requested output to satisfy a type check.\nEvery initial task must lead to the designated terminal and participate in F,\nincluding every conditional branch before evidence permits partial evaluation.\nIndependent requested facts must converge: use a record with explicit required\noutput_contract.fields, or a terminal compose task that consumes all of them and\nanswers the ENTIRE original question. Do not select one independent leaf as the\ncomplete answer. A set is appropriate for a homogeneous requested list, not for\nhiding distinct requested fields. A value-mode record MUST declare its fields.\nIndependent attributes of the SAME already-known entity at the SAME entity/time\nscope MUST share ONE retrieval task with multiple separately typed variables\nwhose demand is that task. Use a record exposing every requested field only for\na requested multi-field factual answer; boolean/one_reason queries retain their\noriginal output contract and legal if/and expressions even when retrieval is\nshared. Split\nretrieval tasks when an actual grounding/reasoning dependency or different\nrequested scopes requires separation; do not invent dependencies to separate\notherwise independent attributes. Keep genuine DAG input dependencies, all\nforeseeable downstream tasks with unbound inputs, and genuine conditional\nbranches intact before evidence allows F_t to eliminate unnecessary work.\nFor a quantity with a unit, prefer text preserving the complete quantity, or a\nsourced record with explicit value and unit fields. If numerical operations are\nrequired, a numeric declaration's unit MUST use the schema's declared unit enum;\nnever invent another unit code or silently discard a unit to fit a bare number.\nIf an expression references a separate unit variable, declare that variable's\ntype, demand and scope in the initial program; never reference an undeclared var.\nDuring structural repair preserve recognized retrieval questions and output\ncomponents. Slot renaming is allowed with corresponding dependency updates; do\nnot delete tasks, expression inputs or record fields to make validation pass.\nComplete legal initial-plan example for the fictional question \"Identify the\nmaker, housing material, and catalogue year of the fictional Lumen C3 sensor.\"\nAll three factual values below are UNBOUND; no answer is supplied or guessed.\n{\n  \"steps\": [{\n    \"question\": \"Identify the maker, housing material, and catalogue year of the fictional Lumen C3 sensor.\",\n    \"output_slot\": \"sensor_details\", \"answer_type\": \"record\",\n    \"inputs\": [], \"execution\": \"retrieval\"\n  }],\n  \"final_node_id\": \"sensor_details\",\n  \"outpu